    {
      "id": "8hlLw19CSNMKfSumoCNV4",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1913.9440631977557,
      "y": 1913.091341663042,
      "width": 748.7480091659047,
      "height": 560,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dashed",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bO3",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1762243893,
      "version": 447,
      "versionNonce": 1057666715,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "OfSTmm23jE4kC8BRuf7ct",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1787919402637,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The Mechanism: Path Rewriting & Stripping\nInside Kubernetes, this process is called Path Rewriting or URL Rewriting. The internal API\ngateway acts as a translator between public-facing, domain-organized URLs and internal,\nservice-specific endpoints.\n\nYour example breaks down mechanically like this:\nStep Attribute Value / Action\n1      Incoming Public URL           https://domain.com\n2     Ingress Action                  Terminates TLS + Validates JWT\n3     Gateway Matching Rule        Detects the prefix /api/ai-service/\n4     Routing Target                  Discovers internal K8s service named ai-service\n5     Rewrite Execution              Strips /ai-service from the path and prepends /api\n6     Forwarded Internal URL       http://cluster.local\n\nWhy this is critical for Enterprise Architectures\n\nDoing this URL translation provides three major benefits to your backend development teams:\n\nDecoupled Code: The developers writing the ai-service code do not need to hardcode context\npaths like /ai-service/ into their application routers. They can just listen to standard roots\nlike /api/createsummary or /createsummary.\n\nEncapsulation: External clients don't need to know how your internal services are named, split,\nor organized. You can change your backend service name from ai-service to llm-orchestrator-v2\nin GKE, and as long as you update your rewrite rule, the client's URL stays exactly the same.\n\nSecurity Isolation: Malicious actors cannot guess your internal cluster directory or network\nlayout because the internal paths are completely abstract from the outside world.",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The Mechanism: Path Rewriting & Stripping\nInside Kubernetes, this process is called Path Rewriting or URL Rewriting. The internal API gateway acts as a translator between public-facing, domain-organized URLs and internal, service-specific endpoints.\n\nYour example breaks down mechanically like this:\nStep Attribute Value / Action\n1      Incoming Public URL           https://domain.com\n2     Ingress Action                  Terminates TLS + Validates JWT\n3     Gateway Matching Rule        Detects the prefix /api/ai-service/\n4     Routing Target                  Discovers internal K8s service named ai-service\n5     Rewrite Execution              Strips /ai-service from the path and prepends /api\n6     Forwarded Internal URL       http://cluster.local\n\nWhy this is critical for Enterprise Architectures\n\nDoing this URL translation provides three major benefits to your backend development teams:\n\nDecoupled Code: The developers writing the ai-service code do not need to hardcode context paths like /ai-service/ into their application routers. They can just listen to standard roots like /api/createsummary or /createsummary.\n\nEncapsulation: External clients don't need to know how your internal services are named, split, or organized. You can change your backend service name from ai-service to llm-orchestrator-v2 in GKE, and as long as you update your rewrite rule, the client's URL stays exactly the same.\n\nSecurity Isolation: Malicious actors cannot guess your internal cluster directory or network layout because the internal paths are completely abstract from the outside world.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
